WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.360
Setting up native Android push notifications usually means wrestling with Firebase Cloud

2
00:00:05.360 --> 00:00:08.120
messaging directly in Kotlin or Java.

3
00:00:08.120 --> 00:00:13.320
It requires untangling intensive background services and configuring the Android manifest

4
00:00:13.320 --> 00:00:16.800
just to get a single message to appear on the device.

5
00:00:16.800 --> 00:00:21.960
When you switch to Expo, the underlying architecture doesn't actually change.

6
00:00:21.960 --> 00:00:27.840
Firebase Cloud Messaging still handles the delivery and Android still draws the alert on screen.

7
00:00:27.840 --> 00:00:33.400
This simply gives you a JavaScript API to control those exact same native mechanisms.

8
00:00:33.400 --> 00:00:38.560
To execute this setup, you need an initialized Expo project, the Expo notifications package

9
00:00:38.560 --> 00:00:43.560
installed, and an emulator or physical device running Android 8 or newer.

10
00:00:43.560 --> 00:00:48.320
Knowing that you are operating the exact same native levers through JavaScript lets you configure

11
00:00:48.320 --> 00:00:53.400
the entire pipeline reliably, avoiding the usual guesswork of cross-platform notification

12
00:00:53.400 --> 00:00:54.400
routing.

13
00:00:54.400 --> 00:00:58.920
A traditional Android developer configures builders and channels using Kotlin.

14
00:00:58.920 --> 00:01:05.000
In our React native ecosystem, we handle those exact responsibilities using the methods provided

15
00:01:05.000 --> 00:01:07.800
by the Expo notifications package.

16
00:01:07.800 --> 00:01:10.400
Look at this side-by-side comparison.

17
00:01:10.400 --> 00:01:16.160
Native Android calls Firebase Messaging for a token, while Expo exposes Get Expo push-token

18
00:01:16.160 --> 00:01:19.400
async to achieve the exact same result.

19
00:01:19.400 --> 00:01:24.840
Starting to local alerts, the native notify function maps to Expo's schedule notification

20
00:01:24.840 --> 00:01:26.400
async method.

21
00:01:26.400 --> 00:01:32.560
The most critical rule inherited from native Android development involves notification channels.

22
00:01:32.560 --> 00:01:37.720
Starting with Android 8.0, the operating system requires all notifications to be assigned

23
00:01:37.720 --> 00:01:42.160
to a specific channel, which governs rules like sound and vibration.

24
00:01:42.160 --> 00:01:47.400
You define this rule set in your JavaScript by calling set notification channel async,

25
00:01:47.400 --> 00:01:52.040
defining a unique string ID, and specifying the importance level before you ever attempt

26
00:01:52.040 --> 00:01:53.760
to send a message.

27
00:01:53.760 --> 00:01:58.440
If you skip this critical channel creation step, the Android system will silently drop your

28
00:01:58.440 --> 00:02:01.000
notification payloads immediately.

29
00:02:01.000 --> 00:02:05.400
The app won't crash, but the user will never see the alert.

30
00:02:05.400 --> 00:02:09.960
Translating these native rules into JavaScript ensures the Android operating system accepts

31
00:02:09.960 --> 00:02:15.520
and displays your notifications exactly as it would for an app written purely in Kotlin.

32
00:02:15.520 --> 00:02:20.320
Before fetching any identifiers, you first call request permissions async.

33
00:02:20.320 --> 00:02:24.680
This triggers the standard Android system dialogue, asking the user to allow notifications

34
00:02:24.680 --> 00:02:26.480
from your application.

35
00:02:26.480 --> 00:02:30.960
Once the user grants permission, you immediately invoke the token retrieval method.

36
00:02:30.960 --> 00:02:35.480
This requests a unique identifier from the Firebase Cloud Messaging servers, routing it back

37
00:02:35.480 --> 00:02:36.920
down to your app.

38
00:02:36.920 --> 00:02:40.360
The result of that function is a long alphanumeric string.

39
00:02:40.360 --> 00:02:44.680
This is your FCM token, safely extracted into your JavaScript environment.

40
00:02:44.680 --> 00:02:47.560
You store this exact string in your backend database.

41
00:02:47.560 --> 00:02:51.480
It acts as the precise mailing address for this specific device, allowing your servers

42
00:02:51.480 --> 00:02:53.120
to target it remotely.

43
00:02:53.120 --> 00:02:55.920
You don't always need a remote server to ping the user.

44
00:02:55.920 --> 00:03:01.320
To schedule a local alert, your app only needs to calculate the timing and construct the message

45
00:03:01.320 --> 00:03:04.080
content entirely on the client side.

46
00:03:04.080 --> 00:03:10.720
By passing a title and a body and a trigger condition into schedule notification async,

47
00:03:10.720 --> 00:03:16.200
you instruct the Android OS to generate a standard heads-up alert at that exact moment.

48
00:03:16.200 --> 00:03:21.120
You can expand this basic alert by passing additional parameters in the content object.

49
00:03:21.120 --> 00:03:26.320
This allows you to apply native features, like big text style for longer paragraphs, or

50
00:03:26.320 --> 00:03:30.560
inject large images directly into the expanded notification view.

51
00:03:30.560 --> 00:03:35.160
You can also include a badge property in that same configuration, which commands the Android

52
00:03:35.160 --> 00:03:40.600
launcher to update the small unread indicator number resting on your application's icon.

53
00:03:40.600 --> 00:03:45.080
Following up these local triggers allows the application to surface reminders and updates

54
00:03:45.080 --> 00:03:50.880
based on the user's on-device activity, avoiding unnecessary network requests entirely.

55
00:03:50.880 --> 00:03:54.400
Getting the notification onto the screen is only half the process.

56
00:03:54.400 --> 00:03:59.760
You also need to determine how the app responds when the user actually engages with it.

57
00:03:59.760 --> 00:04:05.440
When the app is actively open on the screen, you use Add Notification Received Listener.

58
00:04:05.440 --> 00:04:10.520
This intercepts the incoming data payload silently, so you can update the UI without throwing

59
00:04:10.520 --> 00:04:13.640
a disruptive alert over the user's current task.

60
00:04:13.640 --> 00:04:18.160
If the app is minimized or closed, the OS handles the visual alert.

61
00:04:18.160 --> 00:04:23.320
In this state, you register Add Notification Response Received Listener to catch the interaction

62
00:04:23.320 --> 00:04:27.400
when the user eventually taps that alert in the notification tray.

63
00:04:27.400 --> 00:04:32.680
Managing these two separate listeners replicates how a native Kotlin app uses Android Intense

64
00:04:32.680 --> 00:04:37.040
to route data differently depending on the application's current lifecycle state.

65
00:04:37.040 --> 00:04:39.800
This flow chart shows the data extraction path.

66
00:04:39.800 --> 00:04:42.800
When the user taps, the listener receives an object.

67
00:04:42.800 --> 00:04:48.360
You access the custom JSON payload by navigating through the notification, into the request, down

68
00:04:48.360 --> 00:04:52.440
to content, and finally accessing the data object.

69
00:04:52.440 --> 00:04:57.040
Extracting this payload data allows you to read specific ID numbers or routes attached to

70
00:04:57.040 --> 00:05:02.120
the alert, giving you the information needed to immediately navigate the user to the exact

71
00:05:02.120 --> 00:05:04.680
relevant screen inside the app.

72
00:05:04.680 --> 00:05:09.360
You have successfully connected the native Android Firebase cloud messaging infrastructure to your

73
00:05:09.360 --> 00:05:14.760
react native environment, controlling channels, tokens, and payloads through a single JavaScript

74
00:05:14.760 --> 00:05:16.320
interface.

75
00:05:16.320 --> 00:05:20.480
This chart visualizes our progression through the native resources module.

76
00:05:20.480 --> 00:05:25.080
We've mapped out permissions, accessed the camera and file storage, tapped into hardware

77
00:05:25.080 --> 00:05:29.360
sensors, and finally integrated background notifications.

78
00:05:29.360 --> 00:05:34.080
The next sequence of technical modules focuses strictly on application performance.

79
00:05:34.080 --> 00:05:39.280
You will profile react renders, optimize flat list rendering, and tune the Hermes engine

80
00:05:39.280 --> 00:05:42.200
to ensure maximum frame rates.

81
00:05:42.200 --> 00:05:47.160
Integrating these device level features gives your application its core functionality, but

82
00:05:47.160 --> 00:05:52.120
applying rigorous performance optimizations is what will elevate it to a professional standard.

